      "bytes": 8987,
      "sha256": "3b31fc0acbd05ba0ecc0fce6cff04346d29e7e6d74c7e4c490aeba43abd8dc2c",
      "text_excerpt": "# Signed direction, executable costs and currency-news inputs\n\nCompleted September 11 Eastern / September 12 UTC. This work adds tested research code and a reproducible comparison. **Probability calibration improved; dependable profitable direction is still unestablished.** The principal numerical failure in the selected decisions is overestimating the signed price move, rather than badly estimating the endpoint exit spread.\n\n## What was implemented\n\n- Reused the preceding 68-pair technical/peer/news panel and existing gradient-boosting lineage. Compared technical24 and combined108 fields, adding known asymmetric entry costs and fixed pair identity. This does not claim that all historical 200+ feature sets are active or that boosting is new.\n- Fitted direct signed means and a probability/conditional-magnitude decomposition at **5, 15, 30 and 60 minutes**. Positive and nonpositive branches use squared-error mean targets and the same underlying sample weights, including flat returns. Separate models estimate long and short exit costs. Eight retained bundles contain 48 estimators and eight probability calibrators.\n- Corrected a limitation of the older opportunity ranker's proxy: probability of cost clearance times a median magnitude and conditional direction is not the unconditional signed expected return. All three mean estimates are retained for comparison; no winner or threshold was chosen after seeing these results.\n- Added separate-day monotone probability calibration and a fixed cost decision: choose the greater expected long/short return only above a predeclared 1 bps margin; otherwise wait. Forecast values remain available when the decision is to wait.\n- Added a four-head research curve consumer with original reference/target identity, asymmetric bid/mid/ask costs, strict quote/feature/model clocks, remaining-price arithmetic and explicit refusal reasons. It does not refit the conditional forecast when the quote changes. It is separate from the existing 13-head curve and H1 practice manager, and has no order interface.\n- Built a strict reader for the older currency meter and a source-pinned collector for new observations. Two local captures preserve exact article versions, exclusions, 21 currency states and real post-commit visibility. A fresh state projects into 66 descriptive pair fields. The collector is currently a one-shot tool, with no persistent service installed.\n\n## What the measurements say\n\nThe fixed comparison assessed **39,893 pair/time/horizon rows** over September 10 and 11, after September 7\u20138 fitting and separate September 9 calibration. All labels mature strictly before the next partition. These dates were already inspected: this is discovery on reused history, not untouched confirmation.\n\nCalibration improves Brier score and log loss in all eight input-group/horizon aggregates and all 16 corresponding daily comparisons. Scores remain close to an uninformative 50% probability. Better probability scores do not consistently improve selected returns.\n\nOf the 24 learned group/estimate/horizon combinations, **17 have negative selected net returns, four positive, and three select nothing**. The six-row positive 5-minute case spans only three decision times. The positive combined 15-minute cases have 41\u201343 rows over 12 decision times and lose on September 10. The larger positive 30-minute case averages only +0.148 bps and becomes negative with an additional 1 bps of cost. These do not establish a qualified replacement.\n\nFor example, the combined 30-minute direct model overestimates selected signed returns by 5.977 bps, while its selected exit-cost error contributes \u22120.039 bps: it slightly overestimates costs. The calibrated technical 60-minute mixture has 2.439 bps of return optimism and \u22120.094 bps of exit-cost error. This identifies where the forecast-to-decision estimate fails; it does not prove why the predictive relationship is weak.\n\nAll 24 learned means lose to zero on absolute error, although that metric"
    },
